
Title:

Ontology-Driven Data Integration and Contract-Based Framework for Autonomous Decision Support in Low Earth Orbit Systems

Selling Point (Customer Perspective):

An intelligent, explainable framework that fuses heterogeneous space-domain data and applies contract-based reasoning to enable autonomous decision support for next-generation satellite, drone, and ground operations.

🔹 1st Quadrant — Problem Definition

What are you trying to do?
Develop an integrated digital framework that unifies LEO mission data from multiple sources and enables trusted, autonomous decision-making through formalized design and run-time contracts.

How is it done today?
Current space missions rely on siloed data streams (thermal, comms, ops, environment) with manual analysis and disconnected verification pipelines. Decisions rely heavily on human interpretation of reports, telemetry, and models.

What are the limits of current practice?

No standardized way to integrate or reason across multi-source, multi-domain datasets.

Lack of explainable decision logic for autonomy and mission assurance.

Data fusion and model verification occur post-facto, not in real time.

Battle management systems lack semantic traceability and contract-level verification.

Relation to Seedling Solicitation:
Aligns with calls for disruptive digital engineering, autonomous operations, and trusted AI for space systems.

🔹 2nd Quadrant — New Idea

What is your idea?
Create a contract-based ontology-driven data integration framework that enables autonomous decision support for LEO systems.
The approach connects physics-based models, telemetry, and knowledge from documents into a machine-interpretable knowledge graph with LLM-assisted reasoning and run-time contract monitoring.

Novelty:

Merges ontology-based data fusion with contract-based system design.

Introduces autonomous decision support contracts — executable representations of operational intent and constraints.

Integrates LLMs for knowledge extraction and real-time situational awareness.

Value beyond current practice:

Enables explainable, traceable autonomy.

Reduces decision latency and human workload.

Harmonizes engineering and operational data into one semantic layer.

Assumptions:

Ontologies can represent system constraints across domains.

CRAD and NASA are seeking tools for explainable autonomy in space.

Key Risks & Mitigation:

Risk: Complexity of integrating diverse data sources.
→ Mitigation: Start with one thermal-fluid + comms dataset and scale modularly.

Risk: LLM reliability in high-stakes reasoning.
→ Mitigation: Contracts serve as validation layer for AI outputs.

Why it will succeed:
Leverages RTX’s enterprise ontology infrastructure, AI research strengths, and ongoing collaborations in digital thread and autonomy.

🔹 3rd Quadrant — Impact

CRAD Agencies Interested:

NASA – Digital Twin & MBSE for autonomous mission ops.

AFRL – Resilient space architectures and autonomous decision aids.

DARPA – Battle management and human-AI teaming initiatives.

Qualitative Difference:
Transforms data into structured, explainable knowledge for rapid, reliable decisions across LEO missions.

Quantitative Benefit:

30% reduction in data-to-decision cycle time.

Improved operational assurance and verification confidence.

Foundation for autonomous mission replanning and fault recovery.

Transition Path to RTX BUs:

Raytheon Space: battle management, satellite autonomy.

Collins: avionics and comms data integration.

Pratt & Whitney: digital twin and performance monitoring.

BU SMEs Engaged:
Early discussions with Raytheon autonomy and digital thread teams; Collins OCKMS and PW Digital Twin groups.

🔹 4th Quadrant — Path Forward

Seeding (5 months / $120K):
Develop a working prototype for data-to-decision pipeline using thermal-fluid and mission telemetry datasets.

Milestone 1 (Midterm):
Ontology and knowledge graph integrating sample LEO system data; initial contract schema defined.

Milestone 2 (Final):
Prototype demonstrating autonomous decision support: LLM-assisted contract extraction, run-time contract checking, and decision traceability dashboard.

CRAD Marketing Strategy:
Engage NASA’s MBSE and AI offices, AFRL’s Space Vehicle Directorate, and DARPA’s Strategic Technology Office for potential follow-on.

Partners for Follow-on CRAD:

Universities: USC (Madni Lab), MIT AeroAstro, University of Austin.

Labs: JPL, Sandia, Lincoln Lab.

Industry: Stardog, Palantir, or Anzo for data fusion tooling.
